   "id": "pmt/pure-core-2/ch4/q1",
   "question": "<figure class=\"practice-figure\" style=\"max-width:30mm;margin:2mm auto\"><img src=\"resources/pmt/pure-core-2-ch4/q01-fig-1.png\" alt=\"Figure 2: a gold pendant in the shape of a teardrop, pointed at the top and rounded at the bottom, with a vertical arrow beside it marked 2 cm for its height\"></figure><p>Figure 2 shows the image of a gold pendant which has height 2 cm. The pendant is modelled by a solid of revolution of a curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> about the <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"y\"></span>-axis. The curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> has parametric equations</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"x=\\cos\\theta+\\frac12\\sin2\\theta,\\quad y=-(1+\\sin\\theta)\\qquad0\\leqslant\\theta\\leqslant2\\pi\" data-display=\"true\"></div><ol class=\"parts\" type=\"a\"><li><p>Show that a Cartesian equation of the curve <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> is</p><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"x^2=-\\left(y^4+2y^3\\right)\" data-display=\"true\"></div><span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></li><li>Hence, using the model, find, in <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm{cm}^3\"></span>, the volume of the pendant. <span class=\"marks\">(4)</span></li></ol>",
   "steps": [
